From 3857131ec6d8397738b1c22177ed62ec5de048c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Shine <mark@ankra.io>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:17:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(loadbalancer): let backend members use the node's
 private-network address

A managed load balancer reaches its backends over the SDN private network
it is attached to, but members are built from the node's Kubernetes
InternalIP. Those are normally the same address, and on a cluster where
they are not, the result is silent: the API accepts the members, the
service is assigned an EXTERNAL-IP and the controller logs
EnsuredLoadBalancer, but no member ever passes a health check and the
load balancer serves 503 or refuses connections.

This bites any cluster that advertises an overlay address as its node IP
- for example a WireGuard mesh joining nodes across zones, where kubelet
runs with --node-ip set to the mesh address. The load balancer is not a
mesh peer, so every backend is unreachable.

Adds loadBalancerBackendAddressSource to the CCM config, defaulting to
node-internal-ip so existing deployments are byte-for-byte unchanged.
Set to private-network, each member's address is instead read from the
node's server interface on the load balancer's own private network - the
one address that is reachable by construction. The controller already
fetches server details to pick that network, so the lookup reuses the
existing helper; addresses are resolved once per request rather than per
service port.

An unknown value is rejected at startup rather than silently falling back,
since the failure it would cause is invisible at runtime.
